average cost. I will HOLD the current position to allow for recovery, as the fundamentals are still elite. Actually, looking at the prompt \"Decide ONE action\", and the goal is a 6-12 month horizon. The fundamentals are so strong that selling now would be a mistake.\n\n    *   Signal: HOLD\n    *   Confidence: 0.85\n    *   Reasoning: NVDA''s fundamentals remain elite with a forward P/E of 14.76 and 85.2% revenue growth, despite the current price being below the SMA50 and SMA200. The RSI of 37.97 suggests a temporary pullback, providing a favorable entry point for the existing position to recover over a 6-12 month horizon.\n\n    *Wait, let me re-evaluate.* If I''m a \"decision engine\" for a \"long-only equity portfolio\", and the price is down but fundamentals are great, \"HOLD\" is the standard. If I wanted to be aggressive, I''d \"BUY\" more. But \"HOLD\" is the most logical response to a position that is currently underwater but fundamentally sound.\n\n    *Wait, let''s look at the price vs SMA200.* $190.01 vs $192.80. It''s very close. The RSI is low. This is a classic \"buy the dip\" scenario. However, since I already hold the position, \"HOLD\" is the most conservative and correct action for a long-only portfolio unless I want to increase the weight.\n\n    Let''s check the prompt again: \"Decide ONE action\".\n    If I HOLD, I keep the 18.8235 shares.\n    If I BUY, I open a position (but I already have one, so I can''t \"open\" one).\n    The prompt says: \"BUY - open a position (only if you do not already hold one)\".\n    Since I *already* hold one, I *cannot* BUY.\n    Therefore, the only options are HOLD or SELL.\n    Given the strong fundamentals (Forward P/E 14.76, 85% growth), SELL is incorrect.\n    Therefore, the answer must be HOLD.\n\n    *Self-Correction:* The prompt says \"BUY - open a position (only if you do not already hold one)\". This implies I cannot \"BUY\" more. I can only \"HOLD\" or \"SELL\".\n\n    *Final check of data:*\n    Price